David Damerell wrote:
> Quoting  Ray  <bear@sonic.net>:
>>Doug Freyburger wrote:
>
>>>NetHack has been declared mature code according to its sourceforge page.
>
>>Fundamental advances in the game, at this point, would require a different 
>>underlying architecture, design, and data structures.
>
> I'd like some convincing explanation of why you wouldn't have said that,
> if anyone had asked you, three days before the release of 3.1.0.

I have two different answers, neither absolute -

1) On the sourceforge web site it says Nethack is a mature project.  To
anyone who has taken software engineering courses at a university it
means something specific when the maintainer is the one who makes that
declaration.

2) If you graph the historical dates it fits a software lifecycle curve.
Since the release of  3.1.0 the history was earlier in the curve.

And one that's been a joke in the software industry for so long I first
read it recited over a deck of punched cards -

3) The first version of a program teaches you what you wanted it to do,
so the rewrite to version 2 includes significant changes in behavior.

The second version of a program teaches you how you want to do it so
version 2 has very little performance improvement over version 1.

The third version of a program teaches you that the background
technology has moved to the point the program is in obselescence but
version 3 performs much better than version 2.

Versions four and on should therefore be expected to be maintenance
releases.

It's an old joke but it too expresses the same sentiment about the
software lifecycle.

I've been played rogue a few times and I played plenty of Hack in the
1980s.  I love Nethack and I have no plans to stop playing just because
it has not seen a new release in a long time.  Should I switch to one of
the variants?  Probably.  I'm in no hurry.


